      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood's name was adopted in 1969, uniting several early 1900s additions that began development just after 1900 and were largely complete by 1930.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heritage_Hills%2C_Oklahoma_City",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Overholser Mansion, built by Henry Overholser, is a Châteauesque-style house museum that opened to the public with guided tours.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heritage_Hills%2C_Oklahoma_City",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The annual Heritage Hills Historic Homes and Gardens Tour started in 1967, drawing about 500 ticket buyers and media attention, and continues as a fall tradition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://heritagehills.org/history/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The neighborhood showcases a mix of architectural styles, from Châteauesque and Italian Renaissance Revival to Prairie School and Dutch Colonial Revival.",
